# 2.3. Characterization

The morphologies and sizes of materials were inspected by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Tecnai G20, FEI, USA) and field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, ZEISS Sigma 500). The crystalline structure of materials was determined by X-ray diffractometry (XRD, D8A23, Bruker, Germany) with a detection range from 3 to \(80^{\circ}\) and a step size of \(0.02^{\circ}\). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) images were recorded on a spectrometer (NIGOLET iS10). The nitrogen adsorption and desorption curves of the materials at \(77~\mathrm{K}\) were determined by Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) analysis (BK112T). The UV spectra were recorded with an ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectrophotometer (UV-3600, Shimadzu, Japan). X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were obtained with an ESCALAB 250Xi instrument (Thermo Scientific, USA). Inductively-coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometry (ICPAES) was conducted with an Optimal 8000 instrument (Perkin Elmer, US). Room temperature photoluminescence (PL) measurements were performed on a fluorescence spectrometer (LS-55, PE, USA).

# 2.4. Photothermal effect test

The photothermal effect of the materials was monitored with a thermal imager (Testo 875i, Testo Instruments International Trading (Shanghai) Co., Ltd) by irradiating \(200~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) of different samples (phosphate buffer saline (PBS), PB, PCN-224, and PB-PCN-224) with \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) LED light \((0.3~\mathrm{W/cm}^{2})\) for \(15~\mathrm{min}\). The temperature change of the materials was monitored with a thermal imager every three minutes and an image was captured. A thermal imager was used to monitor the temperature change of the PB-PCN-224 for three cycles of \(15~\mathrm{min}\) heating and \(15~\mathrm{min}\) cooling.

# 2.5. Electrochemical test

Samples \((4.0~\mathrm{mg})\) were dispersed in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol and then \(800~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) naphthene was added and dispersed by ultrasonication. Subsequently, \(50~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the resulting solution was dropped onto a piece of titanium \(6~\mathrm{mm}\) in diameter. Titanium flakes coated with sample were heated at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30~\mathrm{min}\). A standard three-electrode apparatus, with a platinum sheet and an \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) electrode as the auxiliary and compared electrode, was used to study the photocurrent of the materials. The titanium plates deposited with materials were used as the working electrode in a CHI 660E electrochemical station (Shanghai Chenhua, China) equipped with the illumination source \((660~\mathrm{nm}\) light). The three electrodes were inserted into a beaker filled with \(0.5~\mathrm{M}~\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) electrolyte. The photoreaction of the prepared photoelectrode (i.e., I-t) was measured for \(180~\mathrm{s}\) by testing the photocurrent densities under a short-cut light illumination (light on/off cycle: \(30~\mathrm{s}\)) at a declinational potential of \(0.5~\mathrm{V}\) vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\).

# 2.6. Photodynamic test with 1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran (DPBF) and electron spin resonance spectroscopy (ESR)

The reaction of DPBF with \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduces the fluorescence intensity centered at \(420~\mathrm{nm}\). Samples \((200~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) were first dispersed in DPBF solution (dissolved in dimethyl sulfone) and followed by irradiation under \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) red light \((120~\mathrm{s}\), LED light, \(0.3~\mathrm{W/cm}^{2}\)) and analysis with an enzyme marker. The ESR spectra were recorded on a JES-FA200 spectrometer. TEMP \((50~\mathrm{mM})\) was used as a spin trap for the detection

of singlet oxygen during irradiation of samples by light. Spin traps were ESR silent but formed stable radicals with an ESR signal after donating electrons. Determination of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) by electron paramagnetic ESR using 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline-N-oxide (DMPO) as the spin-trapping agent in water, because \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) could react with DMPO rapidly to obtain DMPOOH.

# 2.7. Antibacterial test

The antibacterial capacities of the materials were assessed using S. aureus (ATCC 29 213) and S. aureus biofilm through a spread plate method. Firstly, \(160~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of bacterial solution \((1 \times 10^{7}~\mathrm{CFU~mL}^{-1})\) was mixed with the sample solution containing either \(40~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of PBS or \(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) of PB, PCN-224, or PB-PCN-224 and the mixtures were transferred to a 96-well plate in two groups. The first group was illuminated for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) with the \(660~\mathrm{nm}\) light, while the second group was incubated in the dark for \(15~\mathrm{min}\). After the illumination, \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of bacterial solution was extracted from each well and diluted 100-fold with Luria-Bertain (LB) medium. A \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) volume of the diluted bacterial samples was applied to agar plates and cultured at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\).

The antiseptic effectiveness against S. aureus biofilm was determined as follows. A \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) volume of bacterial culture was co-cultured in 96-well plates for \(48~\mathrm{h}\) at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The bacterial suspension was replaced with \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of sterile Luria–Bertani (LB) medium every \(12~\mathrm{h}\). The plates with S. aureus biofilm were then washed with sterilized PBS \((\mathrm{pH} = 7.4)\) and covered with \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the different materials (PB, PCN-224, and PB-PCN-224; \(1~\mathrm{mg/mL}\)). The antibacterial steps above were then repeated. The bacterial colonies on the plates were photographed, and the antibacterial rate was determined by counting the number of colonies using the following formula (where A is the number of colonies):

Antibacterial efficiency \((\%) = (\mathrm{A} \text{ in control group} - \mathrm{A} \text{ in experimental group})/\mathrm{A} \text{ in control group} \times 100\%\)

# 2.8. Bacterial morphology

The bacterial morphologies were examined by SEM after the antibacterial experiment. The bacteria were fixed by removing the bacterial slurry and adding \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}~2.5\%\) glutaraldehyde to the 96 well-plate and reacting for \(2~\mathrm{h}\). The bacteria were then dehydrated sequentially in a graded ethanol series for \(15~\mathrm{min}\). The bacterial morphologies were examined by SEM after drying.

# 2.9. Fluorescence staining of Live/Dead bacteria and detection of intracellular reactive oxygen species

Live/Dead Bacterial Viaticity Kit (Molecular Probes, L-13152) was employed to evaluate the antibacterial ability of materials. Both live (green) and dead (red) bacteria were observed by a fluorescence microscope (IX73, Olympus, USA). DCFH-DA was used to detect the intracellular reactive oxygen species.

# 2.10. Protein leakage

The bacterial membrane damage was detected using bichinchonic acid (BCA) protein detection. At the end of the antibacterial experiment, \(150~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of bacterial liquid was mixed with \(150~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) PBS, and the mixture was centrifuged. A \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) sample of the suspension was added to \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of BCA reagent, and protein leakage was measured with a microplate reader.

# 2.11. In vitro cell experiment

The mouse fibroblast cell line (NIH-3T3, Tongji Hospital, Wuhan) was used in these experiments. The cells were co-cultured with samples
for 1 day. Samples containing NIH-3T3 cells were washed with sterile PBS, and the cells were immobilized with \(4\%\) formaldehyde for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), followed by washing with PBS. FITC (YiSen, Shanghai) was then added for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) to stain the cells, which were then washed with PBS. DAPI (YiSen, Shanghai) was then added for \(30\ \mathrm{s}\) to dye the cells, followed by washing with PBS. Images were captured from an inverted fluorescence microscope.

The 3-[4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl]-2,5-diphenyl tetrazoliumbromide (MTT) assay was used to assess the cytotoxicity of PB-PCN-224 \((200\,\upmu\mathrm{g}/\) mL), PB \((200\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL)}\), and PCN-224 \((200\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL)}\). NIH-3 T3 cells were cultured in 96-well plates in cell nutrient medium for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The medium was then removed and new medium containing the samples \((200\ \upmu\mathrm{L})\), with a sample to medium ratio of 1:9) was added and culture was continued for a further \(24\ \mathrm{h}\). The culture medium was then replaced with MTT solution \((200\ \upmu\mathrm{L},\,0.5\ \mathrm{M})\) at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After \(4\ \mathrm{h}\), the MTT solution was removed and \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of DMSO solution was added to the well and placed for \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) on a shaker with continuous rotation. A \(100\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) volume of the supernatant was removed and its absorbance (OD) was measured at \(490\ \mathrm{nm}\) in a microplate reader.

# 2.12. In vivo animal wound healing test

Male Wistar rats \((180{-}200\ \mathrm{g}\) body weight) were provided by Beijing Weitong Lihua Experimental Animal Technology Co., Ltd. The Institute of Radiation Medicine, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences authorized the test. The rats were housed singly in cages for 3 days and then randomly divided into the following three groups with three rats per group: control group (PBS), \(3\ \mathrm{M}\) group (routine wound treatment, Minnesota Mining and Manufacturing Medical Equipment (Shanghai), Ltd), and test group (PB-PCN-224). After anesthesia with \(16\%\) chloral hydrate, the backs of rats were slashed and \(20\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) diluted bacterial solution \((10^{8}\ \mathrm{CFU/mL})\) was added to the wound, along with \(20\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of either PBS (control group and \(3\ \mathrm{M}\) group) or \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) of PB-PCN-224 (test group). After illuminated with \(660\ \mathrm{nm}\) light \((0.3\ \mathrm{W/cm}^{2})\), the wound in the control group was bandaged with opaque sterile medical tape. The \(3\ \mathrm{M}\) group was treated with a standard \(3\ \mathrm{M}\) wound dressing applied to the wound. The wounds of the experimental group were tightly wrapped with nontransparent sterile medical tape. The rats were then housed at standard temperature.

The wounds were photographed at 2, 4, and 8 days after treatment. The tissues were dyed with Giemsa and hematoxylin/eosin (H&E) stains to assess the quantity of bacteria around the wounds and the course of wound healing. The primary organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney) were harvested on day 8 and stained with H&E to evaluate the in vivo biotoxicity of the materials.

# 3. Results and discussion

# 3.1. Characterization

The TEM images of the samples showed pristine PB particles with an average diameter of \(260\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1a) and a spherical structure of the PCN-224 samples with a uniform size distribution of about \(70\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S1), which were well accordance with the corresponding SEM images (Fig. S2a, S2b). The TEM image of PB-PCN-224 composite showed that PCN-224 had grown around the PB nanoparticles, possibly due to the presence of PVP molecules on the surface of the PB nanoparticles (Fig. 1b and Fig. S2c). As shown in Fig. S3a, the \(0.50\ \mathrm{nm}\), \(0.36\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.24\ \mathrm{nm}\) lattice fringe spacing represented the (200), (220) and (420) plane of PB.[34] After attaching PCN-224, the lattice of PB had not changed (Fig. S3b). Possibly, the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) groups in the PVP molecules had coordinated with zirconium ions [35], thereby facilitating the growth of PCN-224 on the surface of the PB nanoparticles. Moreover, as shown in Fig. S4, the zeta potentials of PB, PCN-224 and PB-PCN-224 were \(-23.2\), \(-11.1\) and \(-11.2\ \mathrm{mV}\) respectively, and the zeta potential data of PCN-224 and PB-PCN-224 were basically the same, indicating that PCN-224 grew around PB.

# PCN-224 grew around PB.

A crystal structure diagram for PB-PCN-224 was showed in Fig. 1c. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) data confirmed that PB-PCN-224 contained all the elements those in PB and PCN-224, indicating the co-existence of the two kinds of MOFs (Fig. S2d, S2e, S2f). The subsequent TEM mapping further demonstrated the successful synthesis of PB-PCN-224 (Fig. 1d). When PCN-224 was grown on the surface of PB, zirconium appeared and was mainly distributed around PB, with a location of the element showing the same distribution was the same as that of PCN-224. X-ray diffraction (XRD) charts were used to establish the crystallographic structures of the materials (Fig. 1e). The observed diffraction peaks were consistent with previous reports and indicated a very high crystallinity of the PB [35]. When compared with PB alone, the PB-PCN-224 composite demonstrated the diffraction peaks of PB as well as peaks at \(2\theta=4.591^{\circ}\), \(6.494^{\circ}\) and \(7.956^{\circ}\) which respectively represent the (002), (022) and (222) crystal planes of PCN-224 (Fig. S5) [36].

The basic structures of the PB, PCN-224, and PB-PCN-224 composites were further examined by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) (Fig. 1f). In the spectra, the peak at \(2090\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) represented the stretching vibration of the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) bond in the PB nanoparticles, and the peak at \(499\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) represented the bending vibration of \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{Fe}\) [35]. In pure PCN-224, the asymmetric vibration absorption of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) bond in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}\) appeared at \(1701\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [37]. The peaks around at \(1600-1500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were attributed to the stretching oscillation of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{C}\) bond in the benzene ring [36]. The \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H}\) oscillating vibration of the pyrrole ring formed a triplet state belt at 1020, 985, and \(966\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [37]. All the main characteristic peaks of PB and PCN-224 appeared in the FT-IR spectrum of the coupled PB-PCN-224 hybrid material.

The nitrogen adsorption and desorption experiment revealed the porosity of the material. As shown in Fig. 1g, the specific surface area of PB-PCN-224 was \(47.131\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), smaller than that of PCN-224 \((61.395\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\), but the pore sizes of PB-PCN-224 and PCN-224 were both \(1.614\ \mathrm{nm}\), confirming that the addition of PB could reduce the specific surface area of PB-PCN-224 (Fig. S6). Since the size of the pore remained essentially unchanged, the presence of PB did not appear to affect the pore size distribution.

The elementary composition of PB-PCN-224 was determined by XPS analysis. The Fe 2p XPS spectra showed that the binding energies of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) were situated at 708.7 and \(721.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. S7a), while the peak at \(713.07\ \mathrm{eV}\) could be attributed to partially oxidized \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) [38]. The three primary peaks of the N 1s indicated the existence of three chemically different types of nitrogen atoms \(\mathrm{=N-}\) (399.3 eV), \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{N}\) \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{4-}\right)\) (397.4 eV), and \(-\mathrm{NH}\) (396.8 eV) (Fig. S7b) [39,40]. The C 1 s spectrum revealed the presence of \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C}\) (284.2 eV), \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) (284.6 eV), \(\mathrm{O}=\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) (288.4 eV), and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{N}\) (285.5 eV) (Fig. S7c) [41]. The O 1s spectrum was shown in Fig. S7d. The \(\mathrm{Zr}\) 3d spectrum showed the binding energies of 182.6 and \(184.7\ \mathrm{eV}\), belonging to \(\mathrm{Zr}\ 3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and \(\mathrm{Zr}\ 3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\), respectively (Fig. S7e) [42].

# 3.2. Photoelectrochemical characterization and band structure

The UV–visible diffuse reflectance spectra were recorded to evaluate the optical properties of the samples. Fig. 2a shows the absorption of PCN-224, which was mainly in the visible region, but its absorption was relatively weak. The addition of PB significantly increased the absorption of PB-PCN-224 in the visible region, particularly at \(500-800\ \mathrm{nm}\). Therefore, PB-PCN-224 had a greater light absorption capacity than PCN-224. The photoluminescence (PL) of the materials was primarily due to the recombination of the photoinduced electrons and holes. Generally, the possibility of this photogenic electron-hole pair recombination of this material decreased as the fluorescence intensity decreased, indicating a better photocatalytic effect of this material [43]. The PL spectrum in Fig. 2b showed lower fluorescence intensity for PB-PCN-224 than for the other samples. This might reflect the fast shift of electrons from PB to PCN-224 down the heterojunction, which would
